+\section{Diagnosing the Scope of Skill Utility}
+\label{sec:skillsbench-experiments}
+
+The first diagnostic question is where a completed skill is useful. A positive source-task result can arise from content that applies across instances or from a procedure fitted to the original task. We distinguish these possibilities by first examining paired utility in two standard-task runs, then deploying frozen skills under three forms of task variation. Inspection of skill content and execution traces connects the resulting utility profiles to decisions made during deployment.
+
+\subsection{Experimental Setup}
+
+We evaluate SkillEvolver~\cite{zhang2026skillevolver}, SPARK~\cite{zhou2026spark}, and CoEvoSkills~\cite{zhang2026coevoskills} on SkillsBench~\cite{li2026skillsbench}, with two runs per method. Each run pairs an execution using the generated or evolved skill with a no-skill execution of the same task. Both runs of each method use the same task set: 81 tasks for SkillEvolver, 84 for SPARK, and 85 for CoEvoSkills.
+
+We report mean verifier reward on a 0--100 scale and the mean within-task difference between the skill and no-skill conditions. For a run with $N$ tasks, the paired gain is
+\begin{equation}
+\Delta = \frac{100}{N}\sum_{i=1}^{N}
+\left(r_i^{\mathrm{skill}}-r_i^{\mathrm{no\text{-}skill}}\right).
+\end{equation}
+We also count tasks with positive, negative, and zero differences.
+
+\begin{table*}[t]
+\centering
+\small
+\setlength{\tabcolsep}{7pt}
+\begin{tabular}{llrrrrrrr}
+\toprule
+\textbf{Method} & \textbf{Run} & \textbf{Tasks} & \textbf{No skill} & \textbf{Skill} & $\boldsymbol{\Delta}$ & \textbf{Improved} & \textbf{Regressed} & \textbf{Tied} \\
+\midrule
+SkillEvolver & 1 & 81 & 30.20 & 55.81 & $+25.61$ & 27 & 6 & 48 \\
+SkillEvolver & 2 & 81 & 37.32 & 62.34 & $+25.02$ & 26 & 3 & 52 \\
+\midrule
+SPARK & 1 & 84 & 17.82 & 19.66 & $+1.84$ & 9 & 8 & 67 \\
+SPARK & 2 & 84 & 14.37 & 20.81 & $+6.45$ & 13 & 5 & 66 \\
+\midrule
+CoEvoSkills & 1 & 85 & 42.21 & 52.44 & $+10.24$ & 17 & 9 & 59 \\
+CoEvoSkills & 2 & 85 & 23.54 & 33.31 & $+9.76$ & 17 & 10 & 58 \\
+\bottomrule
+\end{tabular}
+\caption{Two runs per method on SkillsBench. Scores are mean verifier rewards multiplied by 100; $\Delta$ is the mean paired gain in percentage points. Each method uses an identical task set across its two runs.}
+\label{tab:skillsbench-six-runs}
+\end{table*}
+
+\input{skillsbench-variant-table}
+
+\subsection{Source-Task Gains Recur across Runs}
+
+All three methods have positive mean paired gains in both runs (Table~\ref{tab:skillsbench-six-runs}). SkillEvolver gains 25.61 and 25.02 points, and CoEvoSkills gains 10.24 and 9.76 points. For both methods, the aggregate advantage is similar across runs even though absolute rewards change substantially. SkillEvolver's no-skill mean rises from 30.20 to 37.32, while CoEvoSkills' falls from 42.21 to 23.54. A comparison of skill-condition scores alone would obscure this pattern. The within-task contrast locates the observed benefit relative to what the executor achieves without the artifact in each run.
+
+Task-level outcomes clarify how those means arise. SkillEvolver improves 27 tasks and regresses six in run~1, followed by 26 improvements and three regressions in run~2. CoEvoSkills improves 17 tasks in each run, with nine and ten regressions. SPARK has positive gains of 1.84 and 6.45 points, but its first run has nearly balanced improvements and regressions (nine versus eight), whereas the second has 13 improvements and five regressions. A repeated positive mean can therefore coexist with different concentrations of benefit and harm.
+
+The common finding is a recurring aggregate benefit on the source tasks, with heterogeneous effects across individual tasks. The next diagnostic question is which parts of this usefulness survive task changes. The next comparison holds the source skill fixed and changes the task, so that the executor must reuse existing content rather than receive a skill evolved for the new instance.
+
+\input{skillsbench-variants}
